WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.609
Our land burned down five years ago, and what
is mainly growing back, are these mimosa trees.

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:07.637
Or, also known as acacia.

00:00:07.637 --> 00:00:11.832
If nothing is done to the land,
it's likely that catches fire again.

00:00:12.637 --> 00:00:18.321
To have a better fire break and more space,
we want to clear the area behind Basekamp.

00:00:18.321 --> 00:00:23.425
We have to chop a bunch of mimosa trees,
but we realized we don't know much about them.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.323
So, we are going to visit an expert,
that studied them for 20 years.

00:00:28.323 --> 00:00:31.483
And also, try to build things with them ourselves.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:35.480
Welcome to a new Project Kamp update.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.788
So, as you can see, here is the kitchen container.

00:00:39.788 --> 00:00:43.070
And, here is the mimosa forest, where it starts.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:47.885
We already started chopping down here,
but there is a lot to go still.

00:00:47.885 --> 00:00:52.083
So, before, we couldn't start this work, 
because we were not allowed to use power tools.

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:54.200
Everything was super dry.

00:00:54.502 --> 00:00:59.471
The soil is now super wet, rain started,
 and we are now allowed to start full on.

00:00:59.471 --> 00:01:03.497
It's a super dense bushy area, so it's difficult to go through.

00:01:03.497 --> 00:01:05.760
A lot of fallen trees around...

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.583
And, if we find a native tree, we for sure keep it.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.148
So, this is a public road, and where we'll mark 
the end of our cutting section.

00:01:13.148 --> 00:01:18.267
This is one of the edges, but we will just leave 
a bit of a line of trees.

00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:20.057
So, we're protected a bit from the view.

00:01:20.057 --> 00:01:21.364
And this, is the other edge.

00:01:21.364 --> 00:01:24.423
So, we opened this last year from the lake, up till Basekamp.

00:01:24.423 --> 00:01:30.000
So, we could water the plants,
 and we will stop until here.

00:01:31.055 --> 00:01:32.221
This is the team.

00:01:32.221 --> 00:01:35.170
It will be a bit fluctuating, 
because it will be rainy.

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:37.483
And, people will come, and go all the time.

00:01:37.483 --> 00:01:39.320
But, we're ready to start!

00:02:22.645 --> 00:02:23.476
- What are you doing?

00:02:23.703 --> 00:02:28.556
Trying to compress your wood, so it takes up less space.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.459
We've been clearing the patch for a couple of days now.

00:03:48.459 --> 00:03:51.714
Hard work, but still cannot see much change.

00:03:51.714 --> 00:03:53.514
Lots of bushes and trees.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:58.715
Anyway, our main reason to chop down the mimosas,
is to reduce fire risk.

00:03:58.715 --> 00:04:04.240
Our neighbor Antonio experienced the fire in 2017,
 and he has it cleared.

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:18.741
So, we are taking Antonio's advice.

00:04:18.741 --> 00:04:25.498
We will clear a 10-meter security area around our Basekamp, 
before we continue with the rest of the patch.

00:04:34.335 --> 00:04:37.298
Careful! Oh my god!
- Run, run, run, run, run!

00:05:46.448 --> 00:05:50.615
This guy you see cutting mimosas hasn't been
properly introduced yet.

00:05:50.615 --> 00:05:54.557
He's the youngest in our group, full of energy, and fun!

00:05:56.961 --> 00:06:01.197
Hey! I'm Tasman, 21 years old, and freshly imported from Australia.

00:06:01.197 --> 00:06:03.942
Just like my mate the mimosas, but not so invasive.

00:06:03.942 --> 00:06:08.611
I've been traveling for a couple years, and come with 
a strong passion for plants and native species.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:13.919
I came to Kamp to expand my knowledge on construction,
 and sustainable land management.

00:06:14.194 --> 00:06:19.057
Outside of Kamp, I enjoy challenging myself, 
and exploring natural spaces.

00:06:19.739 --> 00:06:24.523
In the future, I'd like to move more into natural
 resource management, but also still lots to learn.

00:06:24.523 --> 00:06:27.352
Alright-y, enough talking! Let's get back to work.

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:40.490
As good as it's gonna get!

00:06:43.025 --> 00:06:44.265
That's fun.

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:57.380
Where was it? 
- In the brambles.

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:26.538
Some little mushroom...

00:07:26.538 --> 00:07:28.672
Hello! Welcome to autumn!

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:43.444
At the moment, the space around the lake, is quite narrow.

00:07:43.444 --> 00:07:45.040
It's hard to access it.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:49.508
This is why we will take away these mimosas,
dead trees, and rumbles in between.

00:07:49.508 --> 00:07:53.677
So, we create open space around the lake, to take on next steps.

00:08:20.745 --> 00:08:22.427
We finally made our way to the lake.

00:08:23.174 --> 00:08:27.838
We have a clear patch, from the back of the container, 
up until here now.

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:32.845
The next step is gonna continue at the back.

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:52.954
In the middle of all of these mimosas, that we have been clearing out,
we found a native tree and we've drawn on it.

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:21.400
So, most of the place now is cleared.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:24.480
We made a few different organized piles.

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:29.622
This is pile number one, with small burnt sticks: they will be chipped.

00:10:29.622 --> 00:10:36.304
This is pile number two, which is burned wood as well,  
but too thick to be wood chipped.

00:10:36.304 --> 00:10:38.104
And, this is pile number three.

00:10:38.104 --> 00:10:43.040
It's the top of the fresh mimosas,
 that will also be for the wood chipper.

00:10:43.425 --> 00:10:45.171
This pile doesn't have a number.

00:10:45.171 --> 00:10:47.002
We don't really know what to do...

00:10:47.002 --> 00:10:54.497
It's the pile of the brumbles, and it's not so cool if it's still on the floor, 
because it just gets stuck in our clothes.

00:10:54.497 --> 00:10:56.008
We made it into a pile.

00:10:56.008 --> 00:10:59.598
We can't really make fires at the moment,
and we don't know what to do with it.

00:10:59.598 --> 00:11:01.613
For now, it just lays here.

00:11:01.613 --> 00:11:04.783
These two piles are the ends of the fresh mimosas.

00:11:04.783 --> 00:11:08.433
Because they are fresh, it's not very easy 
for the wood chipper to chip them.

00:11:08.433 --> 00:11:10.143
And, they are also a bit too thick...

00:11:10.143 --> 00:11:15.621
So, we've separated them into different sizes, and they will be used
for different projects as you will see.

00:11:15.621 --> 00:11:20.303
Although mimosa is all over Portugal, 
it seems that people don't make stuff with them.

00:11:20.303 --> 00:11:25.171
The wood is not very strong, but we will do 
some experiments to find some applications.

00:11:25.171 --> 00:11:30.881
Trying different techniques like carving, weaving, 
landscaping, or carpentry.

00:11:31.043 --> 00:11:33.496
Let's start with Martijn and Emma.

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:37.978
So, for our mimosa project, we're gonna do some wood carving.

00:11:37.978 --> 00:11:41.857
I am going to make a wash powder measuring spoon.

00:11:41.857 --> 00:11:43.801
I'm gonna make a back scratcher.

00:11:43.801 --> 00:11:48.939
For my carving, I'm going to use a hatchet, carving knife, my spoon knife...

00:11:49.621 --> 00:11:55.696
My chisels most of the time, and a wooden hammer.

00:11:55.696 --> 00:12:01.376
But, I saw that Frank is using it at the moment, 
so I think I'm gonna make one myself real quick.

00:12:01.376 --> 00:12:02.717
Also from mimosa trees.

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:04.620
Let's start!
- Let's go!

00:12:06.198 --> 00:12:07.815
This is for my hammer...

00:12:10.801 --> 00:12:11.801
Is your hammer ready?

00:12:12.873 --> 00:12:13.639
What do you think?

00:12:53.603 --> 00:12:54.963
Ah, it feels good!

00:12:54.963 --> 00:12:55.946
A nice sharp knife.

00:12:56.760 --> 00:13:02.110
I'm going to use my spoon carving knife now,
to make the hollow space in the spoon.

00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:07.870
We've got all these cultural differences, 
about how much washing powder should be used.

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:12.040
We've got this American guy,
who says you have to load the whole thing.

00:13:12.040 --> 00:13:13.891
They always like to go big, you know?

00:13:13.891 --> 00:13:18.840
We've got this German guy, who says it should be 98.5.

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:19.955
They're really precise.

00:13:19.955 --> 00:13:25.809
So, to settle this matter, I called my mother, 
and she said it should be 110.

00:13:25.809 --> 00:13:27.636
So, we're going for that...

00:13:27.636 --> 00:13:29.202
- Your mother? 
- My mother, yeah...

00:13:29.559 --> 00:13:30.607
- Gender roles!

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:18.409
I think I'm finished with the spoon.

00:14:18.409 --> 00:14:20.156
It's got a nice scoop...

00:14:20.156 --> 00:14:21.359
Nice interior...

00:14:21.359 --> 00:14:27.646
I gave a little bit of sanding on the edges, to smoothen them, 
but I'm gonna leave all these cutting marks.

00:14:27.646 --> 00:14:29.322
I like the aesthetics of that.

00:14:29.322 --> 00:14:32.226
Now that the wood carving is done, it works actually.

00:14:32.226 --> 00:14:33.077
It's nice.

00:14:35.695 --> 00:14:37.486
Oh, that felt good, eh?

00:14:37.486 --> 00:14:38.137
Pound it!

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:47.230
Hey, Emma!

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:48.783
Ah! You also finished your piece.

00:14:48.783 --> 00:14:50.344
- Yeah! You too, I see.

00:14:50.344 --> 00:14:52.017
Wow! That's a nice back scratcher.

00:14:52.017 --> 00:14:54.805
- Yeah, you did a nice job too, man!

00:15:00.536 --> 00:15:01.338
Hey there!

00:15:01.338 --> 00:15:04.068
I'm Rita, and I volunteer here at Project Kamp.

00:15:04.068 --> 00:15:10.079
My mimosa project is a weaved basket, 
 made out of the bark of the mimosas.

00:15:10.079 --> 00:15:15.379
This was our first prototype -- our first experiment with weaving.

00:15:15.379 --> 00:15:21.225
For this one, we decided to go thinner, 
so the strips are half the width.

00:15:21.225 --> 00:15:27.221
I think the other ones were around four centimeters,
and these ones are around two centimeters, I would say.

00:15:27.221 --> 00:15:31.045
And, we doubled the amount of strips, because of that.

00:15:31.045 --> 00:15:35.860
We went from having 12 strips, so 6 by 6, to 24.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:13.617
The main principle of weaving is: you have vertical lines of material.

00:16:14.202 --> 00:16:15.962
And then, horizontal lines.

00:16:16.547 --> 00:16:22.231
I'm just going to see where the last strip is.

00:16:22.231 --> 00:16:30.459
So, if it was under, then this one has to be over, 
and this one is over, so I go under.

00:16:30.459 --> 00:16:33.393
And just repeat the process.

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:53.040
It's always good to have a spray bottle in hand.

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:58.175
So that you can mist that your working in.

00:17:59.442 --> 00:18:01.760
Or, even the basket itself.

00:18:03.058 --> 00:18:08.160
And, make it more flexible, so that you can shape it.

00:18:27.180 --> 00:18:28.681
Our basket is finished,

00:18:28.681 --> 00:18:34.523
It's been three or four days since I weaved it, and that you saw the final result.

00:18:34.523 --> 00:18:36.553
Now, it's pretty dry.

00:18:36.553 --> 00:18:38.066
It's not going anywhere.

00:18:38.066 --> 00:18:47.129
I kind of like how rough it is, so I don't really mind that there is
 some irregularity, and the shape isn't totally squared.

00:18:47.640 --> 00:18:53.372
The most important thing: it's waste free,
 so you are not purchasing any materials.

00:18:54.054 --> 00:18:57.811
It's a zero kilometer material supply...

00:18:58.233 --> 00:19:06.069
Maybe, this would be a really cool research module for you guys to join in, 
and maybe give some other ideas for weaving.

00:19:06.069 --> 00:19:13.619
Looking forward to find more uses for the mimosa bark,
 because we seem to have an infinite supply of them.

00:19:13.619 --> 00:19:17.240
Let's see what else happens in the meantime.

00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:24.090
So now, we have this beautiful pizza table.

00:19:24.090 --> 00:19:26.840
That we also use as a workspace, during the week.

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:35.222
But, it turns out that most of our chairs are too low,
and we cannot work at a comfortable position here.

00:19:36.522 --> 00:19:41.120
So, I thought it would be nice to have a bar stool, 
to be able to do that.

00:20:58.230 --> 00:21:00.440
Number one is ready, three more to go.

00:21:26.733 --> 00:21:33.386
I'm going to cut the top to the final height, which--

00:21:34.133 --> 00:21:36.170
Hey guys! I'm making a video here!

00:23:13.417 --> 00:23:18.028
The stool itself is ready now, but as you can see, 
it's not very comfortable yet.

00:23:18.028 --> 00:23:23.480
It needs a seat, and let's see if Charlie and Rita can do something about that.

00:23:56.529 --> 00:24:02.933
Now that the stool is finished, Martijn can sit at the proper height,
 with a straight back and work happily.

00:24:07.023 --> 00:24:07.823
All right.

00:24:07.823 --> 00:24:10.000
We're next to the community center...

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:11.897
Down on this slippery hill...

00:24:11.897 --> 00:24:16.973
And, Liz and I are going to set up some steps, 
from the mimosa cut near Basekamp.

00:24:16.973 --> 00:24:18.451
So, let's see how we go!

00:25:55.640 --> 00:25:57.499
Alright, that's the stairs...

00:25:58.441 --> 00:26:00.167
I think that turned out pretty well.

00:26:01.271 --> 00:26:03.977
Hopefully, we can get up the hill a bit easier now, in the rain.

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:08.430
It was good to use some of these mimosas from up the hill.

00:26:08.430 --> 00:26:09.443
Give them another life...

00:26:10.456 --> 00:26:12.485
Yeah, cool!

00:26:13.362 --> 00:26:17.755
So, we've been spending quite a lot of time
with the mimosa trees by now.

00:26:17.755 --> 00:26:21.210
But, we realized we actually don't know too much about them.

00:26:21.210 --> 00:26:24.536
We started looking on the internet to get to know them better.

00:26:24.536 --> 00:26:29.330
In some places, mimose is a symbol for International Women's Day.

00:26:29.330 --> 00:26:33.951
It can have healing properties, and many ecologists like it,
because it grows fast.

00:26:33.951 --> 00:26:38.915
So, it creates a lot of organic mass to build soil,
that retrains water and moist.

00:26:39.240 --> 00:26:41.426
Seems like a useful plant.

00:26:41.426 --> 00:26:45.369
But, most local people around here, actually seem to hate these trees.

00:26:45.369 --> 00:26:49.080
Calling them invasive, useless, and fire risky.

00:26:49.080 --> 00:26:51.203
We wanted to understand more about this.

00:26:51.203 --> 00:26:56.713
So, we went to have a chat, with an expert that has been working
for over 20 years with mimosas.

00:26:56.713 --> 00:26:58.915
To see how he feels about it.

00:27:01.514 --> 00:27:04.329
My name is Manuel Fernandes.

00:27:04.329 --> 00:27:10.033
I started to study acacia's more than two decades ago.

00:27:10.033 --> 00:27:18.543
Mimosa is a common name, that we apply to a
plant that the botanists call "Acacia Dealbata".

00:27:18.543 --> 00:27:22.889
It comes from Australia, from the southeast Australia, from Tasmania.

00:27:23.376 --> 00:27:29.440
There, it is part of the under story of eucalyptus forests.

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:37.746
And, if, some day, the eucalyptus are chopped down, 
or there's a forest fire, or some other disturbance, then...

00:27:37.746 --> 00:27:40.777
The mimosas they find a window of opportunity.

00:27:40.777 --> 00:27:47.326
And, for several decades, they become dominant,
until the eucalyptus grows again.

00:27:47.326 --> 00:27:49.064
They proliferate a lot by seed.

00:27:49.064 --> 00:27:51.614
They produce lots of seeds every year.

00:27:53.646 --> 00:27:58.223
When you chop down one Acacia, it regenerates.

00:27:58.223 --> 00:28:03.059
But also, the root system has the property of emitting new individuals.

00:28:03.059 --> 00:28:08.739
A single stem of mimosa may grow
 more than two meters, per year.

00:28:09.187 --> 00:28:11.190
That's a lot, of course!

00:28:11.190 --> 00:28:23.720
And, they don't have what we may call natural enemies,
as they do on their original area, in Australia and Tasmania.

00:28:26.309 --> 00:28:33.066
Nowadays, it is not easy to use these plants,
because they their use is forbidden by law.

00:28:33.549 --> 00:28:39.336
Only in special occasions, in special situations, we can use them.

00:28:39.336 --> 00:28:47.129
But, I know some experiences of use for making compound,
 for agricultural uses, and forestry uses.

00:28:47.129 --> 00:28:52.703
Also, for making charcoal, it is very suitable, as fire wood.

00:28:52.703 --> 00:29:01.394
And, of course, the bark of these plants is widely used 
for the tanning industry.

00:29:01.394 --> 00:29:05.756
Not any longer in Portugal, but in countries, like South Africa, and Brazil.

00:29:08.279 --> 00:29:15.200
We can't say that it's more or less flammable,
than other species or other types of vegetation.

00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:17.187
Depends on the surrounding landscape.

00:29:17.187 --> 00:29:19.746
When did it rain for the last time?

00:29:19.746 --> 00:29:23.746
Depends on the age of the plants.

00:29:24.040 --> 00:29:26.200
That depends on a lot of factors.

00:29:28.408 --> 00:29:36.475
Most of the people, they have some difficulty
in understanding, that mimosas are proliferating.

00:29:36.475 --> 00:29:41.369
Because of the way we manage our lands.

00:29:41.369 --> 00:29:43.560
They are more like a symptom.

00:29:43.560 --> 00:29:50.328
They are more like a consequence than anything,
than a cause, of ecological change.

00:29:50.328 --> 00:29:54.020
Fire is the trigger, in most of the occasions, but not only fire.

00:29:54.020 --> 00:29:58.980
Every mobilization of the soil, triggers this species.

00:29:58.980 --> 00:30:06.374
I think mimosas, in some occasions, they might even be 
an opportunity, an ecological opportunity.

00:30:06.374 --> 00:30:11.547
The only thing we need is to start to research.

00:30:11.547 --> 00:30:14.156
We feel we know much more about mimosas now.

00:30:14.156 --> 00:30:17.748
We have to deal with them, but also we can use them.

00:30:17.748 --> 00:30:20.512
And, in the future, much more ideas will come along.

00:30:21.167 --> 00:30:26.880
You are also welcome to join the discussion in the research module,
following the link in the description.

00:30:26.909 --> 00:30:30.633
Where you can also find the full interview, with the expert.

00:30:32.740 --> 00:30:35.068
But, still one thing to do to finish the job.

00:30:35.068 --> 00:30:37.603
We called a friend to bring his wood chipper.

00:30:37.603 --> 00:30:41.000
And, we will chip off some piles, to cover our land.

00:31:29.673 --> 00:31:34.950
So, if you want to buy us a container now, or a tractor, 
or a wood chipper, go to support.projectkamp.com.

00:31:34.950 --> 00:31:38.043
So, if you have a tractor laying around,
you can donate it to us.

00:31:38.043 --> 00:31:42.366
Could be a tractor that can handle this 
kind of wood chipper, so we can shred this.

00:31:42.366 --> 00:31:46.880
Get this organic matter, start to create soil, and regenerate our land.

00:31:52.668 --> 00:31:57.016
Finally, the space is completely cleared and we
have easy access to it.

00:31:57.016 --> 00:31:58.240
I will show it to you.

00:31:59.516 --> 00:32:01.700
This is exactly what it looks like.

00:32:01.700 --> 00:32:05.137
It's now very much fire resilient.

00:32:05.137 --> 00:32:09.304
And, the neighbors are super happy, and we are much more safe.

00:32:09.304 --> 00:32:14.100
Wood chips are really brilliant, and we spend the whole day chipping it.

00:32:14.100 --> 00:32:17.176
But we have still loads of piles to go.

00:32:17.176 --> 00:32:21.935
But, for now, we just give back all this organic material to the land.

00:32:23.040 --> 00:32:26.538
There is some water lines, as this place is quite steep.

00:32:26.538 --> 00:32:30.000
This will help to slow down, for the water to run through.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:34.022
And, we'll just keep more water, on this part of the land.

00:32:34.022 --> 00:32:39.470
This lake was aggressively open before,
 so there is a lot of erosion happening.

00:32:39.470 --> 00:32:42.404
And, the path was becoming shorter, and shorter.

00:32:42.404 --> 00:32:48.372
Now, we can just walk kind of easily, and access some places easier.

00:32:53.200 --> 00:32:58.633
We still have loads of piles to process, 
and we have to check how we will do that.

00:32:58.633 --> 00:33:01.071
It was hard work!

00:33:01.071 --> 00:33:04.067
We worked a lot, but it was super fun as well.

00:33:04.067 --> 00:33:06.066
This is how it looks like for now.

00:33:07.320 --> 00:33:13.772
Next week's video, is going to be challenges,
so bring up your questions in the comments for us to answer.

00:33:14.600 --> 00:33:17.280
Thanks for watching, and don't forget to subscribe!

00:33:18.136 --> 00:33:24.231
If you like what we do, you can support us on Patreon,
 and your help would be highly appreciated.

